      },
      "worked": "Let $H(x)=P(x)-x$. Then $H$ has degree 4, $H(x)\\ge0$, $H(1)=H(3)=0$ and $H(2)=1$. Since its graph cannot cross the $x$-axis, both zeros have even multiplicity. Its degree is 4, so both are double roots.\n$$\\begin{align*}H(x)&=k(x-1)^2(x-3)^2\\\\H(2)&=k(2-1)^2(2-3)^2\\\\1&=k\\\\H(4)&=(4-1)^2(4-3)^2\\\\H(4)&=9\\\\P(4)&=H(4)+4\\\\P(4)&=13\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
